+# Traceability Doctrine — the `ch:trace` marker
+
+Every board row, commit, and test-provenance comment in this repository is expected to
+carry one machine-readable line saying **what the work is tied to, and what proves it**.
+That line is the `ch:trace` marker. It is not a summary and it is not decoration: it is a
+claim with named parts, and every part is either a value a reader can resolve or an
+explicit `none:<reason>`.
+
+Audience: **developers** writing the marker, **operators** reading a board row to decide
+whether work is really landed, and **integrators** auditing what shipped.
+
+Status: **documentary**. Nothing in this repository parses, validates, or gates the
+marker (§9). This document is the reference a reader is expected to apply by hand (§7).
+
+Measurement base: everything asserted below was checked in this checkout at
+**HEAD `35d8700d`**, 2026-11-17, against `origin` = `https://github.com/dexdat/SpecLang.git`.
+Where a board or source line number is cited it is the line number **at that HEAD**; board
+ledgers are append-only and lines shift on the next tick, so every citation also gives the
+row id or the command that re-finds it.
+
+## 1. Why a marker and not prose
+
+Prose decays silently. A paragraph written at commit time reads as authoritative forever,
+even after the file it names has moved, the test it cites has been deleted, and the commit
+it references was rewritten out of the branch. Prose also has no fixed vocabulary, so a
+reader cannot distinguish *"there was no test"* from *"the author did not mention a test"*.
+
+A marker fixes both:
+
+1. **Every field is a resolvable value or an explicit `none:` with a reason.** Silence is
+   not a permitted answer. Missing coverage becomes a positive statement a reader can
+   audit instead of a gap they must infer.
+2. **It is written at authorship time**, when the author still knows which run produced
+   the evidence and which judge did or did not run. A marker reconstructed the next day is
+   a rationalisation.
+3. **It is greppable.** `row=`, `verdict=none:`, `commit=none:open` are answerable queries
+   across the whole ledger without reading one prose paragraph.
+
+The rule of thumb: *a marker is not a record that work happened — it is a list of things
+a reader can go and check.* Length is not evidence. The complete example in §6.1 has
+eleven well-formed fields and still asserts that nothing has been delivered.
+
+<a id="marker-contract"></a>
+
+## 2. Marker contract — grammar and rules
+
+A marker is exactly one physical line beginning with the literal token `ch:trace`:
+
+```
+ch:trace row=<id> key=value key=value ...
+```
+
+Formal shape:
+
+```
+marker         := "ch:trace" SP field (SP field)*
+field          := key "=" value
+key            := [a-z][a-z0-9_]*
+value          := concrete-value | none-clause
+concrete-value := one-or-more characters from [A-Za-z0-9._:/#@+,-]
+none-clause    := "none:" reason
+reason         := [a-z0-9-]+
+```
+
+Rules every marker in this repository follows:
+
+| Rule | Why |
+|------|-----|
+| One line, no embedded newlines | The ledgers and `git log` are line-oriented; a wrapped marker cannot be parsed. Ledger JSON escapes newlines, so a marker inside a row is one line before and after serialisation. |
+| `row=` comes first | Lets a cheap `grep -o 'ch:trace row=[^ ]*'` index every marker without knowing the other keys. |
+| Fields separated by a single space; values contain no spaces | Space is the only separator. A value with a space splits into pseudo-fields that no reader expects — commit `55e74569` does exactly that (§8.9). Quote nothing; re-express the value instead. |
+| Keys are lowercase ASCII | Keeps the vocabulary from fragmenting into `Spec=` / `spec=`. |
+| Unknown keys are tolerated | Forward compatibility: `doc=` and `prompt=` (§3.10) were added after the nine core keys. Readers ignore keys they do not know; authors never drop a core key to make room. |
+| A missing key is not `none:` | Omitting a key means *the author did not answer*. `none:<reason>` means *the author answered: there is nothing here, and here is why.* A partial marker can only be read as the weaker of the two. |
+
+Structural check for one marker (used in §7):
+
+```bash
+grep -Eq '^ch:trace row=[^ ]+( [a-z][a-z0-9_]*=[^ ]+)+$' <<< "$marker"
+```
+
+## 3. Field reference — the nine core fields
+
+All nine keys must be present on a complete marker. `row` must be a concrete value; the
+other eight may be `none:<reason>`.
+
+| Field | Question it answers | Concrete forms used here | Reader may conclude from `none:` |
+|-------|--------------------|--------------------------|----------------------------------|
+| `row` | Which board row is this? | `DOC-6`, `DOC-7`, `DF-SPECLANG-1-R4` | not permitted — see §3.1 |
+| `spec` | What design authority does it implement? | `path#anchor`, `path` | the work is **unanchored**: no spec backed it at write time |
+| `wave` | Which dispatch unit produced it? | `<tick-id>#<phase>`, `<lane>/<n>` | a single agent produced it; nothing to reconcile against siblings |
+| `test` | What executable check covers it? | `path`, `path::case` | **uncovered**: the claim rests on evidence and witness alone |
+| `evidence` | What artifact proves a run happened? | `path` (absolute or repo-relative) | no producer-side artifact exists |
+| `witness` | What independent reading confirms the claim? | `path@origin:<path>` | **single-source**: the claim was never checked against anything the author does not control |
+| `verdict` | Did a judge grade it? | `PASS@<run-id>`, `FAIL@<run-id>` | no judge ran; any "verified" language is the author's own |
+| `commit` | Which sha carries it? | 8+ hex short sha | not landed, or not knowable at write time |
+| `memory` | Which DuckBrain key holds the narrative? | `<namespace>/<row>`, `<namespace>:/<key>` | no durable memory was written |
+
+### 3.1 `row`
+
+The board row identifier, and the anchor of the whole marker. Concrete only: a marker
+that cannot be tied to a row is a lead, not evidence.
+
+Observed: `row=DOC-6`, `row=DOC-7`, `row=README-21`, `row=DF-SPECLANG-1-R4`.
+
+Verification: the id must exist in `.coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl`. The ledger mixes two
+spacing styles (`"id": "DOC-6"` on older rows, `"id":"DOC-7"` on newer), so allow for both:
+
+```bash
+grep -cE '"id": ?"DOC-7"' .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl   # → 1
+grep -E '"id": ?"DOC-7"' .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl | jq -r .status   # → complete
+```
+
+A `row=` that resolves to nothing is the most common way a marker becomes worthless.
+
+### 3.2 `spec`
+
+The design authority the change implements: a repository-relative path, optionally with an
+anchor naming the section, symbol, or line.
+
+Concrete values observed, and whether they still resolve at this HEAD:
+
+| Value | Resolves today? |
+|-------|-----------------|
+| `specs/project-layout.spec.dir/src/templates.ts#generateInitialSpec` | yes — file exists; anchor is a symbol in it |
+| `docs/API_REFERENCE.md#MCP-Server` | **no** — the file's heading is `## 3. MCP Server` (line 174), whose slug is `3-mcp-server` |
+| `README.md#what-works-vs-what-doesnt` | yes — README.md's `## What Works vs What Doesn't` |
+| `README.md#run-a-mini-cascade` | yes — README.md's `### Run a Mini Cascade` |
+| `README.md#L83`, `README.md#L89`, `README.md#L440` | resolves only as a reading instruction; see the drift note below |
+| `README.md#layer-0-example-tree` | **no** — README.md has no heading containing `layer-0` |
+| `none:repository-contract-absent` | n/a — the dominant `none:` reason |
+
+Anchor choice is a trade-off. `#L89` is exact today and drifts silently the moment anyone
+edits above line 89; a heading slug survives edits and fails loudly when the section is
+renamed. The two dead anchors above (`#MCP-Server`, `#layer-0-example-tree`) are both
+silent failures of the kind a heading-slug discipline prevents: nothing errored, the
+marker just stopped pointing at anything. **Prefer a heading slug; treat a line anchor as
+valid only for the tick that wrote it.**
+
+`none:repository-contract-absent` is a strong statement, not a shrug: *this repository has
+no spec to point at for this work*, which is why the row is an audit finding rather than an
+implementation. A reader may conclude the change is unanchored and that any later claim it
+"follows the spec" is false until a spec appears.
+
+Verification: `grep -nE '^#+ ' <file>` and match the anchor by slug or heading text.
+
+### 3.3 `wave`
+
+The dispatch unit that produced the work — a tick id, a lane, or a wave label.
+
+Values observed: `speclang-docs-2026-10-27-00-12-05#audit`,
+`speclang-readme-2026-10-27-15-09-36#sweep`, `speclang-readme-2026-10-27-15-09-36#audit`,
+`readme-sweep/1`, and the `none:` forms `none:serial-audit`, `none:serial-readme-tick`,
+`none:serial-worker`.
+
+`none:serial-<role>` states a fact about concurrency — one agent, no parallel siblings —
+so there is no sibling branch to reconcile or double-count. It is not a defect.
+
+**Two things are true about `wave=` and a reader should know both.**
+
+1. Wave manifests exist, but they are not the namespace of `wave=`. `.coding-hermes/waves/`
+   holds one tracked manifest, `.coding-hermes/waves/SpecLang-foreman-2026-11-17-00-01-35.json`,
+   keyed by `tick_id` and listing `workers[]`, `base_sha`, and `merge_order`. A `wave=`
+   value equal to that `tick_id` resolves to it; nothing else does. Do not write a `wave=`
+   value that claims to be a manifest path.
+2. Tick identity in `.coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl` lives under four different keys —
+   `tick` (most common), `tick_label`, `tick_id`, `tick_key`. `row=DOC-6`'s
+   `wave=speclang-docs-2026-10-27-00-12-05#audit` resolves through `tick_label` at
+   events.jsonl:250 — without the `#audit` phase suffix, which no ledger records.
+   `speclang-readme-2026-10-27-15-09-36` and `readme-sweep/1` appear **nowhere** in the
+   events ledger: they are labels with no artifact behind them.
+
+So: a `wave=` that matches a wave manifest, an events `tick*` key, or a commit trailer is
+checkable; the rest is a label. Say which one you wrote.
+
+### 3.4 `test`
+
+The executable check that covers the change.
+
+Concrete forms in use:
+
+- `test=tests/db.test.ts` — whole file
+- `test=tests/project-layout.test.ts::init-to-cascade` — file plus case name; the case is
+  `it("init-to-cascade: emits a block the cascade generator consumes", …)` at
+  tests/project-layout.test.ts:441
+- `test=tests/e2e/spec-to-code.test.ts::E2E` — file plus the suite label
+
+`none:` reasons observed: `none:open-finding` (dominant — every filed audit finding uses
+it), `none:doc-tree-drift-uncovered-by-tests`, `none:docs-claim-no-test-file`.
+
+`none:open-finding` is the honest value for docs and audit work: the row *is* the gap, so
+no test could exist yet. What a reader may **not** do is read it as "tested elsewhere".
+
+Verification: file exists, and the case name appears in it:
+
+```bash
+test -f tests/project-layout.test.ts && grep -n 'init-to-cascade' tests/project-layout.test.ts
+```
+
+### 3.5 `evidence`
+
+The producer-side artifact proving a run actually happened: a transcript, a matrix log, a
+readback JSON.
+
+Observed values:
+
+- `/home/kara/.hermes/stand-in/docs/speclang/docs-audit-2026-11-16-05-02-33.md` — absolute
+  path; readable on the producing host, meaningless on any other
+- `/home/kara/.hermes/stand-in/readme/speclang/evidence/20-link-targets.txt` — same
+- `none:worker-local-until-foreman-verifies` — deferred, with the reason and the owner
+
+Two ports are available and the repository prefers neither by rule yet, so pick
+deliberately:
+
+| Port | Resolvable from | Survives |
+|------|-----------------|----------|
+| absolute path under a home directory (what most markers use) | the producing host only | until the scratch tree is pruned |
+| repo-relative path (`.coding-hermes/evidence/...`) | any clone | as long as the file is tracked |
+
+`.coding-hermes/evidence/` is tracked and currently holds 14 artifacts (for example
+`.coding-hermes/evidence/DF-SPECLANG-1-R4-tier2-cap-verdict.txt`), yet **no marker points
+at it**. When an artifact must outlive the tick, land it there and cite it repo-relative.
+
+**Weak forms, and why they fail.** Commit `55e74569` puts a tool or a board file in this
+field: `evidence=npm test + npx tsc --noEmit runs`,
+`evidence=git ls-remote + depth-1 anonymous clone rc=0`,
+`evidence=board row reasoning (commands + outputs inline)`. Each names the *producer* of
+evidence instead of the evidence: nobody can re-open "npm", and the board reasoning is a
+pointer to a claim, not proof a run happened. If the honest answer is "I ran it and saw it
+pass, and the only record is my word", write `none:not-written` (§4) — not a tool name.
+
+### 3.6 `witness`
+
+The anti-tautology field. `evidence` is what the author produced; a **witness is a reading
+taken from a source the author does not control**. The test is not "is there a second
+file" — it is *"could the author have written this value alone?"* A log the author wrote
+never passes that test, however long it is.
+
+Witness forms, in descending distance from the author:
+
+| Form | What makes it out-of-band | Resolves with | In use here? |
+|------|---------------------------|---------------|--------------|
+| `path@origin:<path>` | the remote's content, not the working tree | `git cat-file -e origin/main:<path>` | **yes** — the only concrete witness on the board/test surface (`row=DOC-9`, `.coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl:126`), and in commit trailers `b9bb5042` / `40fdd74b` (`specs/project.scl`) |
+| `verdict:<run-id>` | the judge tool names the run by a hash of `task_id:evaluated_at`; the author cannot mint it | `git show refs/gitreins/history:.gitreins/history/<date>/<run-id>/verdict.json` | form defined, no marker uses it yet |
+| `tag:<name>@<sha>` | an annotated tag object, created at release time | `git rev-parse <name>^{commit}` | tags exist (`v0.1.0`→`ff96a2e9`, `v1.0.0`→`f7b4ce7d`, both ancestors of `origin/main`); no marker uses it |
+| `ci:<workflow>@<sha>` | a hosted run of `.github/workflows/ci.yml` (workflow name `CI`) bound to that commit | the forge's run record for the sha — Actions UI, or `gh run list --commit <sha>` | no marker uses it yet |
+| `external:<auditor>@<date>` | a human or third-party audit outside this repo | nothing machine-readable here | no marker uses it |
+| `memory:<ns>:<key>@<record-id>` | the store assigns the record id (event 272 records `1d2e750e-fb0a-44d5-9e74-b1c4c99a37d0` for `speclang:/projects/speclang/docs-audit/2026-11-16-05-02-33`) | DuckBrain lookup | form documented in the ledger, not in a marker field |
+
+`none:` reasons observed on rows and in commits, each naming a *specific* absence:
+`none:auditor-probe-only`, `none:static-source-versus-doc-audit`,
+`none:source-versus-repository-docs`, `none:worker-worktree`,
+`none:local-command-output`, `none:no-independent-witness-auditor-probe-only`,
+`none:test-count-varies-by-checkout-provisioning`,
+`none:repository-internal-claim-versus-run`,
+`none:repository-internal-clone-versus-checkout`.
+
+Read them literally. `none:source-versus-repository-docs` says the comparison *was* the
+method, so no second artifact exists — it is a recorded way of being single-source, not a
+second source. `none:local-command-output` says the author's own terminal output is the
+only record, which is evidence, not a witness. `none:test-count-varies-by-checkout-provisioning`
+names a real blocker: the check cannot be re-run to the same number on a differently
+provisioned checkout.
+
+`witness=none:*` on a row that also claims a concrete `verdict` is a contradiction worth
+flagging (§8.3). Honest position at this HEAD: **one** marker on the board/test surface
+carries a concrete witness, and every other one is a recorded absence. That is the gap
+this field exists to expose, not a formality to fill with `none:` and forget.
+
+### 3.7 `verdict`
+
+The judge outcome, if a judge ran.
+
+Concrete form: `PASS@<run-id>` / `FAIL@<run-id>`, where `<run-id>` is the directory name
+under `.gitreins/history/<date>/`, i.e. `sha256(task_id:evaluated_at)[:8]`. Example that
+resolves today: run `449273d5` is `PASS` for `DF-SPECLANG-3-R4`, `pass_class: judged` —
+
+```bash
+git show refs/gitreins/history:.gitreins/history/2026-11-17/449273d5/verdict.json | jq '{task_id,passed,pass_class}'
+```
+
+**No marker in this repository carries a concrete verdict.** Every one is a `none:` form:
+`none:open-finding`, `none:foreman-must-run-judge`, `none:no-judge-ran`. Tier-2 results
+land in `.gitreins/history/` (and on the `refs/gitreins/history` ref) and are not copied
+back into markers. Until that copy-back exists, a `verdict=` field records *intent to
+judge*, not a grading.
+
+Two operational caveats, both measured in this checkout:
+
+- The history ref is **local**. `git ls-remote origin 'refs/gitreins/*'` prints nothing, so
+  a fresh clone has no verdicts until someone runs
+  `git push origin refs/gitreins/history:refs/gitreins/history`. A `verdict=` value is
+  therefore resolvable per-checkout, not repository-wide.
+- A verdict grades a commit that may no longer exist on any branch. Run `449273d5` grades
+  `1354cc85`; that object is in the database but `git branch -a --contains 1354cc85` is
+  empty at this HEAD. The run id is the durable handle; the graded sha is not.
+
+Storage details and the runbook live in `docs/gitreins-verdicts.md`.
+
+### 3.8 `commit`
+
+The sha that carries the work — for a landing row, the landing commit.
+
+Observed forms: 8-hex short shas (`commit=ad80bae0`, `commit=92dd0567`), `none:open` (not
+landed), `none:worker-commit` (structurally unknowable at write time: a trailer is written
+before its own commit exists).
+
+`commit=none:worker-commit` is legitimate and not a licence to leave the field unresolved
+forever — the foreman's merge is where it becomes `commit=<sha>`.
+
+Three distinct meanings get collapsed into this one field in current practice, and a
+reader must decide which is meant before trusting it:
+
+| Intent | Value that expresses it |
+|--------|------------------------|
+| the commit that landed the row | `commit=<landing sha>` |
+| the tree the finding was measured at | say so in prose; do not put it in `commit=` |
+| not landed | `commit=none:open` |
+
+`row=README-21` is the cautionary case: its trailer carries `commit=92dd0567`, but
+`92dd0567` is the *measurement base* ("dogfood: speclang delivery 5 of the same tick …"),
+and the row is still `pending`. `row=DOC-7` is the mirror image: its row text still reads
+`commit=none:open` while the row is `complete` with `commit_hash 9bcea767` (§8.1).
+
+Verification is two steps and both matter — the sha exists, **and** it is an ancestor of
+the branch that is supposed to contain it:
+
+```bash
+git cat-file -t <sha>                                   # exists in this object database
+git merge-base --is-ancestor <sha> origin/main && echo landed || echo NOT-landed
+```
+
+### 3.9 `memory`
+
+The DuckBrain key holding the durable narrative for this row.
+
+Observed forms: `<namespace>/<row>` (`memory=speclang/README-21`), `<namespace>:/<key>`
+(`memory=speclang:/projects/speclang/docs-audit/2026-11-16-05-02-33`), and the `none:`
+reasons `none:not-written`, `none:foreman-writes`, `none:exact-key-not-found`.
+
+The three `none:` reasons are meaningfully different and must not be collapsed:
+`not-written` (the write never happened), `foreman-writes` (deferred to another role by
+design), and `exact-key-not-found` (looked, and the key was absent under the expected name
+— itself a finding about key discipline).
+
+The strongest available `memory=` value pairs the key with the **record id the store
+assigned**, because that id is not something the author can invent:
+
+```
+memory=speclang:/projects/speclang/docs-audit/2026-11-16-05-02-33@1d2e750e-fb0a-44d5-9e74-b1c4c99a37d0
+```
+
+No marker uses that form yet; event 272 in `.coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl` records the
+key and record id in prose instead. A marker cannot be verified against DuckBrain from the
+repository alone — the repo is a pointer, the store is the artifact.
+
+**`none:` here is a claim, not a formality.** `row=DOC-7`/`row=DOC-8` once carried
+`memory=none:no-speclang-namespace`. That was false: `list_namespaces` returns a `speclang`
+namespace and the record exists. Event 272 corrects it, names the false statement, and
+replaces the marker. Asserting absence requires a probe; an empty local list is not
+evidence of absence.
+
+### 3.10 Extension keys (optional, observed)
+
+| Key | Meaning | Observed forms |
+|-----|---------|----------------|
+| `doc` | the repository document under change, when the work targets a doc rather than `src/` or a spec | `doc=README.md`, `doc=docs/API_REFERENCE.md`, `doc=docs/layers.md`, `doc=none:code-task` (absolute paths also appear — see §8.10) |
+| `prompt` | the skill/prompt that drove the tick | `/home/kara/.hermes/skills/coding-hermes/coding-hermes-docs/SKILL.md`, `none:not-a-prompt-change` |
+
+These are additions, never replacements. A marker carrying `doc=` still needs all nine core
+keys; dropping `spec=` because `doc=` is present is the partial-marker failure in §8.5.
+
+<a id="none-convention"></a>
+
+## 4. The `none:` convention
+
+`none:` is the most load-bearing token in the grammar and the easiest to abuse. The reason
+slug must name **why there is nothing**, in a form a reader can act on:
+
+| Reason | Reader's action |
+|--------|-----------------|
+| `open-finding` | Nothing to check yet — the row *is* the gap. Revisit when it closes. |
+| `not-written` | The artifact was never produced. Chase the author, not the store. |
+| `foreman-writes` | Deferred by design to a named role. Verify at that role's close-out. |
+| `worker-commit` | Structurally unknowable at write time. Verify at merge. |
+| `repository-contract-absent` | The premise is missing. Fix the premise before trusting the claim. |
+| `open` | Not landed. Do not go looking for a sha. |
+| `auditor-probe-only` / `worker-worktree` | Single-source by construction. Re-check before merge. |
+| `exact-key-not-found` | Looked and found nothing under the expected name. Fix the key discipline. |
+
+Forbidden reasons, because they are indistinguishable from neglect: `n/a`, `todo`, `later`,
+`none`, `unknown`, `see-above`. If the honest answer is "I did not do this", write
+`none:not-written` — a statement a reader can act on — not `none:n/a`, which reads as noise.
+
+A marker where every field is `none:` is legal and sometimes correct (a newly filed
+finding), but it must then be read as an intention, not a delivery (§6.1).
+
+## 5. Where markers live
+
+Four surfaces; all four are in use in this repository.
+
+| Surface | Location | Real example at this HEAD |
+|---------|----------|---------------------------|
+| Board row | `.coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl`, inside the row's `foreman_note` or `reasoning` text | line 104 `row=DOC-6`; 119 `row=DOC-7`; 126 `row=DOC-9`; 131 `row=README-21`; 135-139 `row=README-22..26` |
+| Board event | `.coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl`, in an event's `detail` | line 268 `row=DOC-7`; 271-272 `row=DOC-7`, `row=DOC-8`; 300 `row=README-22..26` |
+| Commit trailer | the marker line(s) in the commit body | `b9bb5042`/`40fdd74b` (`row=README-21`, `row=README-2`), `ad80bae0` (`row=DOC-7`, `row=DOC-8`), `55e74569` (`row=README-6`, `row=README-9`, `row=README-11`) |
+| Test provenance comment | block comment above a suite, naming where the expectations came from | tests/cli/init-cascade-contract.test.ts:16 (`row=DF-SPECLANG-1-R4`), tests/cli/start-dashboard-contract.test.ts:12 (`row=DF-SPECLANG-3-R5`) |
+
+Census at this HEAD: **33 marker occurrences across 21 distinct row ids** on the
+board-plus-test surface, plus **27 marker lines in commit bodies** at `35d8700d`. The
+commit-body count grows by one for every commit that carries a marker — including the
+commit that introduced this document — so treat the number as a reading, not a constant:
+
+```bash
+grep -R -o 'ch:trace row=[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9._-]*' \
+  .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl .coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl tests/ | wc -l   # → 33
+git log --all --format='%B' | grep -cE '^ch:trace row=[A-Za-z]'                    # → 28 (27 at 35d8700d + this commit)
+```
+
+The board is the canonical home. A commit trailer matters because a commit can be rebased
+or cherry-picked away from its row and the trailer is what travels with the code. A
+test-file marker matters because it is the only surface a reader sees while inside the
+test.
+
+Repair belongs on a **new event or a later trailer** — never a silent in-place rewrite of
+the historical ledger line, which would destroy the record that the defect existed. Event
+272 is the model: it names the falsified field, states the correction, and carries the
+replacement marker.
+
+## 6. Complete worked examples
+
+All three are real, copied from this repository, and every resolvable value in them has
+been checked at HEAD `35d8700d`. Each is a single line.
+
+### 6.1 A filed finding — everything known, nothing delivered
+
+The marker on this repository's own row for this document
+(`.coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl:104`):
+
+```
+ch:trace row=DOC-6 spec=docs/traceability-doctrine.md#marker-contract wave=speclang-docs-2026-10-27-00-12-05#audit test=none:open-finding doc=docs/traceability-doctrine.md prompt=/home/kara/.hermes/skills/coding-hermes/coding-hermes-docs/SKILL.md evidence=/home/kara/.hermes/stand-in/docs/speclang/docs-audit-2026-10-27-00-12-05.md witness=none:source-versus-repository-docs verdict=none:open-finding commit=none:open memory=none:not-written
+```
+
+| Field | Asserts | Reader concludes |
+|-------|---------|------------------|
+| `row` | ties to board row DOC-6 | resolvable — `grep -cE '"id": ?"DOC-6"'` → 1 |
+| `spec` | implements the marker contract defined in this document | resolves to §2's `marker-contract` anchor. When the row was filed on 2026-10-27 that anchor did not exist; this document created it, which is the only reason the value is not a dead reference (§8.10) |
+| `wave` | produced by that audit tick | resolvable via `tick_label` at events.jsonl:250; the `#audit` suffix is not recorded anywhere |
+| `test` | no executable check covers it | uncovered by design; the row is the finding |
+| `doc` | the target is a document | doc work, not code work |
+| `prompt` | the docs skill drove the tick | path is readable on the producing host |
+| `evidence` | an audit transcript exists on the producing host | `test -r` it there; elsewhere unverifiable |
+| `witness` | the method was a source-versus-docs comparison | self-comparison, not origin-resolved |
+| `verdict` | no judge graded it | "verified" would be unsupported |
+| `commit` | not landed | do not search for a sha |
+| `memory` | no DuckBrain key | nothing durable to read |
+
+Eleven fields, all well-formed, and the marker still asserts *nothing has been delivered*.
+Marker length is not evidence of work: a short marker with `commit=<landing sha>`,
+`test=<file>::<case>`, and `witness=path@origin:<path>` is worth more than a long one where
+everything is `none:`.
+
