+## 7. How to verify a marker
+
+Run from the repository root. Each block answers one field, and each was executed in this
+checkout to confirm it works.
+
+```bash
+# Index every marker in the working tree (board rows, events, test comments).
+grep -R -n -o 'ch:trace row=[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9._-]*' \
+  --exclude-dir=node_modules --exclude-dir=.git .
+
+# Every marker that reached a commit body.
+git log --all --format='%H %s' --grep='ch:trace row='
+
+# row: does the row exist on the board (both spacing styles)?
+grep -cE '"id": ?"DOC-7"' .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl
+grep -E '"id": ?"DOC-7"' .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl | jq -r .status
+
+# spec: does the anchor exist in the target file?
+grep -nE '^#+ ' docs/API_REFERENCE.md | grep -i 'mcp'      # → ## 3. MCP Server
+grep -n '^<a id="marker-contract"></a>' docs/traceability-doctrine.md   # → the anchor §2 defines
+
+# wave: does the tick exist in the ledger or as a manifest?
+grep -n 'speclang-docs-2026-10-27-00-12-05' .coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl
+ls .coding-hermes/waves/
+
+# test: does the named case exist?
+grep -n 'init-to-cascade' tests/project-layout.test.ts
+
+# evidence: is the artifact readable on this host?
+test -r /home/kara/.hermes/stand-in/readme/speclang/evidence/20-link-targets.txt && echo READABLE
+
+# witness: does the path exist at origin (not merely in the working tree)?
+git cat-file -e origin/main:specs/project.scl && echo "origin has it"
+
+# verdict: does the run exist on the history ref?
+git show refs/gitreins/history:.gitreins/history/2026-11-17/449273d5/verdict.json | jq -r '.task_id, .passed'
+
+# commit: exists AND is an ancestor of the branch that should carry it?
+git cat-file -t ad80bae0
+git merge-base --is-ancestor ad80bae0 origin/main && echo "landed" || echo "NOT landed"
+
+# structural check: complete marker (row plus at least one more field)?
+grep -Eq '^ch:trace row=[^ ]+( [a-z][a-z0-9_]*=[^ ]+)+$' <<< "$marker"
+```
+
+Three habits worth stating, because each one has bitten someone in this repository:
+
+- **Check ancestry, not existence.** `git cat-file -t <sha>` succeeds for a commit that was
+  rewritten out of every branch — `1354cc85` is exactly that object today (§3.7).
+- **Check the tree, not the message.** A commit subject claiming a row was closed proves
+  nothing; read the committed tree (`git show <sha>:.coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl`) when
+  the claim is about board state.
+- **Check the anchor, not the shape.** A syntactically perfect marker with a dead anchor
+  (§8.10) passes every structural test and still points nowhere.
+
+## 8. Failure modes observed in this repository
+
+Each of these is a real instance, not a hypothetical.
+
+### 8.1 Stale commit
+
+`row=DOC-7`'s row text still says `commit=none:open` while the row is `complete` with
+`commit_hash 9bcea767`: the marker was never re-written after the merge. The mirror case is
+`row=README-21`, whose `commit=92dd0567` names the measurement base rather than a landing.
+And a graded commit can disappear entirely — verdict run `449273d5` grades `1354cc85`,
+which no branch contains at this HEAD.
+
+Detection: `git merge-base --is-ancestor <sha> origin/main`, plus a comparison against the
+row's own `commit_hash`. A `commit=` that fails is a marker about a different repository
+state; re-derive it rather than guessing.
+
+### 8.2 Producer-local evidence
+
+Absolute paths under a home directory (`/home/kara/.hermes/stand-in/...`) are valid on the
+producing host and expire when scratch is pruned. A reader elsewhere cannot resolve them,
+and the evidence must then be treated as `none:`. This is not a lie in the marker — it is a
+portability failure the format does not yet force you to avoid, which is why §3.5 names the
+tracked `.coding-hermes/evidence/` port.
+
+### 8.3 Verdict without a witness
+
+`verdict=PASS` combined with `witness=none:*`. A judge graded the author's *claim*; nobody
+independently checked that claim against origin, so the verdict is a statement about prose
+quality. Treat it as ungraded, and prefer `verdict=PASS@<run-id>` plus `witness=verdict:<run-id>`
+so both the grade and the run are citable.
+
+### 8.4 Bare filename as evidence
+
+`row=DOC-9`'s marker (`.coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl:126`) carries
+`evidence=audit-2026-11-16-05-02-33.refire-2.transcript.log` — a filename with no directory,
+unresolvable from any working directory.
+
+The row was repaired, and the repair is instructive: the marker field itself was never
+rewritten, but the row's `detail.evidence[0].artifacts` and its `foreman_note` pin the
+absolute path `/home/kara/.hermes/stand-in/docs/speclang/audit-2026-11-16-05-02-33.refire-2.transcript.log`
+with its sha256, and the row closed as `complete` under commit `4455371a` with event 368
+`task_completed`. A reader who finds this marker today resolves the artifact **through the
+row**, not through the marker. Repair by enrichment works; it is second-best, and the marker
+is what a reader sees first.
+
+### 8.5 Partial marker
+
+The same `row=DOC-9` marker carries four keys — `row`, `doc`, `evidence`, `witness` — three
+of the nine core fields, with no `spec=`, `wave=`, `test=`, `verdict=`, `commit=`, or
+`memory=` at all. Per §2, a missing key cannot be read as "nothing to declare". The only
+valid reading is *the author answered some questions and not others*, which is weaker than
+any `none:` equivalent.
+
+### 8.6 `none:` soup on a closed row
+
+A row marked `complete` whose marker is all `none:*` records an intention. Either the
+delivery did not happen, or the author never rewrote the marker after it landed. `row=DOC-7`
+is the near-miss: complete, merged, with `commit=none:open` still in the row text (§8.1).
+Neither reading is evidence of completion.
+
+### 8.7 Marker describing a different row than its container
+
+Copy-paste across rows: a commit whose subject names one row while its trailer carries
+another's `row=`. Detection: compare the marker's `row=` against the row whose `commit_hash`
+points at that commit. This is the failure that collapses per-row traceability while every
+individual marker still looks well-formed.
+
+### 8.8 `none:` reason that names a mood
+
+`none:todo`, `none:n/a`, `none:unknown` — see §4. An unactionable reason converts a declared
+gap into an undeclared one.
+
+### 8.9 A value that contains spaces
+
+Commit `55e74569` carries three such markers. The clearest:
+
+```
+ch:trace row=README-9 doc=README.md:412-414,450 evidence=npm test + npx tsc --noEmit runs witness=none:local-command-output
+```
+
+After `evidence=` the parser sees `npm`, `test`, `+`, `npx`, `tsc`, … — five pseudo-fields
+that no reader expects and that silently terminate the real value at "npm". The same commit
+writes `doc=README.md:272-294` (a `doc=` value that is a line range, not a path) and
+`witness=none:local-command-output`. If a value needs a space, the value is wrong: use a
+path, a single token, or `none:<reason>`.
+
+### 8.10 Pointers that do not resolve
+
+Four kinds, all live at this HEAD:
+
+- **Dead heading anchors** — `README.md#layer-0-example-tree` (§6.3) and
+  `docs/API_REFERENCE.md#MCP-Server` (§3.2) name headings that do not exist.
+- **Non-path `doc=` values** — `row=README-18`…`row=README-20` put an absolute path in
+  `doc=`: `/home/kara/.hermes/stand-in/readme/speclang/readme-audit-2026-11-03-15-46-12.md`.
+  `doc=` is the document under change and belongs in the repository; a transcript is
+  `evidence=`.
+- **Dead prompt paths** — `row=README-18`…`row=README-20` cite
+  `/home/kara/.hermes/skills/coding-hermes-readme/SKILL.md`, which does not exist on the
+  producing host; the live path is
+  `/home/kara/.hermes/skills/coding-hermes/coding-hermes-readme/SKILL.md`.
+- **Unresolvable wave labels** — `speclang-readme-2026-10-27-15-09-36` and `readme-sweep/1`
+  appear in no ledger, manifest, or commit subject (§3.3).
+
+### 8.11 A `none:` that asserts a false absence
+
+`row=DOC-7`/`row=DOC-8` carried `memory=none:no-speclang-namespace`. The namespace exists and
+the record was writable; the claim was false and was corrected in event 272 with the key and
+the assigned record id (§3.9). Asserting absence is a claim like any other and needs a probe.
+An empty local listing is not evidence that a store lacks a namespace.
+
+## 9. Where this is enforced today
+
+**Nowhere. The convention is documentary, not machine-checked.** This section was written
+from commands run in this checkout, not from intent.
+
+- **No code parses `ch:trace`.** The plausible enforcement surfaces contain no match:
+
+  ```bash
+  grep -R -n "ch:trace" scripts/ src/ bin/ .github/ .githooks/ package.json config/
+  # no output
+  ```
+
+  Outside the ledgers, this document, and the two test comments, the only other hit in the
+  tree is `.gitreins/tasks.yaml`, which stores a task's acceptance criteria as prose and is
+  gitignored — it neither parses nor enforces anything.
+
+- **No trace or lineage validator exists.** `git ls-files | grep -iE 'trace|lineage'` lists
+  exactly one file — this document. Nothing else in the repository is named for
+  traceability, and no script, hook, or workflow reads a marker.
+
+- **The pre-commit hook does not check markers.** `.githooks/pre-commit` resolves the repo
+  root, exits 0 when `.gitreins/config.yaml` is absent, and otherwise runs `gitreins guard`.
+  Per `.gitreins/config.yaml` the tier-1 steps are `secrets`, `build` (`npm run build`), and
+  `tests` (`npx vitest run` in diff mode), with `lsp`, `static_analysis`, and `assemble`
+  enabled alongside. There is no marker step.
+
+- **CI does not check markers.** `.github/workflows/ci.yml` defines workflow `CI` with two
+  jobs — `build` ("Build, test, and guard": build, tests, coverage upload, conditional lint,
+  GitReins tier-1 guard) and `review` ("Tier 2 LLM review"). The review job runs the
+  `ci-pr-review` task with its canonical eight criteria: type errors, tests for new exports,
+  changelog or doc-only diff, no added TODO/FIXME/XXX, no `console.log`, JSDoc on new public
+  exports, no dead code, and spec coverage matching source changes. None of the eight
+  reference `ch:trace`.
+
+- **`package.json` has no trace script.** The full list is `build`, `dev`, `start`, `test`,
+  `test:watch`, `test:coverage`, `cascade`, `lint`, `clean`, `prepublishOnly`,
+  `build:dashboard`, `dev:dashboard`, `postinstall`, `check:sqlite-binding`,
+  `gen:migrations`.
+
+Consequences:
+
+1. A missing marker fails no gate. A malformed marker fails no gate. A marker naming a dead
+   sha, a dead anchor, or a dead path fails no gate.
+2. Compliance comes from author discipline and from readers running §7 — not from the commit
+   hook. Never read "the commit landed" as "the marker was checked".
+3. Because nothing consumes the format, the format is free to drift. This document is the
+   reference; if practice changes, change it here instead of letting the vocabulary
+   fragment.
+
+What enforcement would look like — deliberately out of scope for this document, and not
+implemented: a guard step ahead of `gitreins guard` that parses the staged commit message,
+requires `row=` plus the eight other core keys, rejects any value containing a space, and
+fails when `commit=none:*` appears on a row whose status is already `complete`. Until such a
+step exists, treat every marker as a claim requiring verification, exactly as §7 describes.
+
+## 10. Author checklist
+
+Before writing a marker:
+
+- [ ] `row=` — copied from the board, not from memory, and confirmed with `grep -cE'"id": ?"<id>"'`.
+- [ ] `spec=` — a real path plus a heading anchor, or `none:` with the reason the authority is absent.
+- [ ] `wave=` — the tick/lane id that something records, or `none:serial-<role>` if you ran alone.
+- [ ] `test=` — `path` or `path::case`, or `none:open-finding` if the row *is* the gap.
+- [ ] `evidence=` — a path you confirmed exists **after** the run finished; repo-relative under
+      `.coding-hermes/evidence/` if it must outlive the tick.
+- [ ] `witness=` — prefer `path@origin:<path>` or `verdict:<run-id>`; if single-source, name the
+      absence with a specific reason.
+- [ ] `verdict=` — concrete only if a judge ran, and then with its run id.
+- [ ] `commit=` — the landing sha if landed; `none:open` or `none:worker-commit` if not; never a
+      measurement base.
+- [ ] `memory=` — the DuckBrain key (plus record id if you have it), or `not-written` /
+      `foreman-writes` / `exact-key-not-found`.
+
+After writing it: run §7 against your own marker. A marker you have not verified is a claim
+you have not earned.
